WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.879
The Olympics in Rio were supposed to be a
coming out party for Brazil, to showcase the

00:00:09.879 --> 00:00:16.150
South American giant, 150 million people,
economic powerhouse, energy powerhouse.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.300
Unfortunately, the reality has changed in
that country in the recent years, slipped

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:26.660
into a recession, demonstrations among anxious
Brazilians, literally running Dilma Rousseff,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.260
the president, out of office.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.130
For the athletes gathering in Rio, they expect
fair competition.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.579
They expect even-handed judges.

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:41.390
Unfortunately, for Brazilians, that's not
the reality, they have had to deal with a

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.380
state that has an oppressive presence in their
lives.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:48.091
It picks winners and losers, it over-taxes
them, it makes it harder for them to do business,

00:00:48.091 --> 00:00:49.860
to create jobs.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.190
And Michel Temer, the interim president, has
an opportunity to change that, to bring in

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:59.600
competition into the system, more capital,
create jobs, and create a better future for

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.000
the Brazilian people.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.899
If he can achieve that, the Brazilian people
can achieve a victory of Olympic proportions.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:10.290
Do you think these Olympic Games will be a
turning point for Brazil or will it be more

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.290
of the same?

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:16.170
Please share your comments and give us your
ideas on other topics that you'd like AEI scholars

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.740
to cover in 60 seconds.

